\begin{equation}\label{ecu1}
		x' = A(t) x^3 + B(t) x^2 + C(t) x
	\end{equation}.” Next, “we suppose that
\begin{equation}\label{ecu1}
		x' = A(t) x^3 + B(t) x^2 + C(t) x
	\end{equation}
 has two or more different invariant curves, and we prove (a) and (b.1).” Then it “focus[es] in the polynomial case to provide the general bound of (b.2.1).” Finally it “apply Darboux's Theory of Integrability to prove the remaining parts of (b.2.1), together with (b.2.2) and (c).”

