\begin{equation}\label{eq:inclusion_of_clopens_of_NN+}
		\Clopen(\NN^+) \inclusion \Open(\NN^+)
	\end{equation}
	of clopen subsets of $ \NN^+ $ into all open subsets.

	To see that \eqref{eq:inclusion_of_clopens_of_NN+} does not admit a left adjoint, recall that a subset $ U \subset \NN^+ $ is clopen if and only if $ U $ is either finite and doesn't contain $ \infty $ or $ U $ is cofinite and does contain $ \infty $.
	Consider the subspace
	\begin{equation*}
		Y = 2\NN \union \{\infty\}
	\end{equation*}
	consisting of even natural numbers and $ \infty $; then $ Y $ is not clopen.
	If the inclusion \eqref{eq:inclusion_of_clopens_of_NN+} admitted a left adjoint $ L $, then there would be a smallest clopen subset $ L(Y) $ containing $ Y $.
	To see that no such clopen exists, note that for each odd natural number $ n $, the clopen $ U_n \colonequals \NN^+ \sminus \{n\} $ contains $ Y $.
	Hence
	\begin{equation*}
		L(Y) \subset \Intersection_{n \textup{ odd}} U_n = Y \period
	\end{equation*}
	Since $ Y \subset L(Y) $, this says that $ L(Y) = Y $; but this is impossible since $ Y $ is not clopen.
\end{warning}

